   "id": "fce8f159-b1db-44d9-879d-99c2e7f6a82a",
   "term": "DVD-Audio",
   "verdict": "MINOR",
   "suggestions": [
    {
     "field": "definition",
     "severity": "Medium",
     "issue": "Definition calls DVD-Audio 'high-resolution PCM audio' and presents 24-bit/96 kHz 5.1 as merely 'limited by the format's ~9.6 Mbps maximum data rate,' implying linear PCM at that rate fits. It does not: web-verified via Wikipedia (en.wikipedia.org/wiki/DVD-Audio) and HomeTheaterHiFi DVD Benchmark Part 6 that uncompressed six-channel 24-bit/96 kHz is ~13.8 Mbps, exceeding the 9.6 Mbps ceiling, so MLP (Meridian Lossless Packing) lossless compression is MANDATORY for high-resolution multichannel (and enables the higher stereo rates within the budget). Stating it as plain PCM within the data-rate limit is technically inaccurate.",
     "suggestion": "Note that high-resolution multichannel (e.g., 24-bit/96 kHz 5.1) requires MLP lossless packing to fit under the ~9.6 Mbps limit, since uncompressed PCM at that rate (~13.8 Mbps) exceeds it. E.g.: 'stores linear PCM, losslessly compressed with MLP (Meridian Lossless Packing) when needed — MLP is required for 24-bit/96 kHz 5.1, whose uncompressed rate exceeds the 9.6 Mbps maximum.'"
    }
